  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday) \nIssued at 609 AM EDT TUE SEP 8 2026\n\nThe models are in generally good agreement for the majority of \nthe extended portion of the forecast. Ridging starts out over the \nsoutheastern CONUS as well as the Desert Southwest, while faster \nand progressive flow dominates across the northern half of the \nCONUS. A notable short wave trough will be aligned from the upper\nGreat Lakes through the middle Mississippi Valley. This feature \nwill cruise through the Ohio Valley and eventually New England by \nFriday. Troughing will be maintained across the Mississippi Valley\nFriday into Saturday, as additional short wave energy moves along\nthe north central CONUS. Meanwhile, a deeper upper level low will\npropagate onshore over the Pacific Northwest. As this feature \nswings east, more prominent ridging will take hold downstream \nacross the southeastern CONUS late this weekend and into early \nnext week. \n\nSensible weather will feature an active start across eastern\nKentucky on Thursday night, with a frontal boundary already in the\narea and convection likely ongoing. The highest probabilities \nwill occur north of KY-80, with perhaps a slightly slower trend \nseen in the latest model guidance. PWATs will peak near 2\", along \nwith the surface boundary aligning more parallel with the mid and \nupper level flow. As such, some locally heavy rainers remain a \npossibility. As instability wanes and the overall forcing \ndiminish, convective coverage should gradually lessen overnight \nThursday night.\n\nThis frontal boundary will linger across our southern locales on \nFriday, with a resurgence of likely PoPs seen generally along and\nsoutheast of KY-80. Highs will retreat closer to normal \ntemporarily. We will still be under the influence of weaker \ntroughing aloft on Saturday. Enough moisture will linger to allow\nfor additional diurnally driven convection. Similar to Friday, \nhigher PoPs will be favored across our southeast. From Sunday \nthrough Tuesday, ridging will be building in aloft, keeping \nconvective coverage more limited. Unfortunately, this will also \nmean heat and humidity.",
